.TH fil 1  fil 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH NAME
fil \- A remote shell that keeps your place: reconnect, detach and reattach
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfil\fR [\fB\-L \fR] [\fB\-R \fR] [\fB\-D \fR] [\fB\-A \fR] [\fB\-a \fR] [\fB\-\-transport\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIHOST\fR> [\fISESSION\fR] [\fIsubcommands\fR]
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH DESCRIPTION
A remote shell that keeps your place: reconnect, detach and reattach
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-L\fR \fI<SPEC>\fR
Listen here, connect from the host: [BIND:]PORT:HOST:HOSTPORT, or PORT for the same port on the host\*(Aqs localhost. A bare port binds loopback; * or an empty BIND is every interface. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-R\fR \fI<SPEC>\fR
Listen on the host, connect from here: [BIND:]PORT:HOST:HOSTPORT, or PORT for the same port on this machine\*(Aqs localhost (not ssh\*(Aqs reverse SOCKS proxy). PORT 0 asks the host for a free port. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-D\fR \fI<SPEC>\fR
A SOCKS proxy here that connects from the host: [BIND:]PORT. Repeatable
.TP
\fB\-A\fR
Relay your ssh\-agent to the session while you are attached (the config\*(Aqs agent = "confirm" stays "confirm")
.TP
\fB\-a\fR
Do not relay the agent, whatever the config says (the last of \-A and \-a wins, as in ssh)
.TP
\fB\-\-transport\fR \fI<MODE>\fR
How to reach the host: auto (the pipe first, then QUIC), pipe (ssh only) or direct (QUIC once attached)
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto
.IP \(bu 2
pipe
.IP \(bu 2
direct
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<FILE>\fR
Read this config file instead of ~/.config/fil/config.toml ($FIL_CONFIG also works)
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
<\fIHOST\fR>
The host: a name or alias for ssh, user@name, or a name from your config
.TP
[\fISESSION\fR]
The session to attach to; it is created if it does not exist
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "fil ls"
List the sessions on HOST
.PP
Usage: \fBfil ls [OPTIONS] <HOST>\fR
.TP
\fIHOST\fR
.SS "fil kill"
End a session: its shell and everything it started are killed
.PP
Usage: \fBfil kill [OPTIONS] <HOST> <SESSION>\fR
.TP
\fIHOST\fR
.TP
\fISESSION\fR
.SS "fil detach"
Detach whoever is attached to a session; it keeps running (inside a session, with no arguments: this one)
.PP
Usage: \fBfil detach [OPTIONS] [HOST] [SESSION]\fR
.TP
\fIHOST\fR
.TP
\fISESSION\fR
.SS "fil forward"
Add a forward to a running session, or remove one. The attached client asks its user before it listens or connects for a forward added this way
.PP
Usage: \fBfil forward [OPTIONS] [HOST] [SESSION]\fR
.TP
\fIHOST\fR
The host the session runs on. Left out inside a session: the session is $FIL_SESSION
.TP
\fISESSION\fR
The session (default: main)
.TP
\fB\-L\fR \fISPEC\fR
Add: listen on this machine, connect from the host (same spec as the attach flag)
.TP
\fB\-R\fR \fISPEC\fR
Add: listen on the host, connect from this machine
.TP
\fB\-D\fR \fISPEC\fR
Add: a SOCKS proxy on this machine
.TP
\fB\-\-rm\fR \fIPORT\fR
Remove the forward that listens on PORT. If a \-L and a \-R share the port, say which with the flag instead: `\-\-rm \-L PORT`
.SS "fil ports"
Show what is listening on the host and what is forwarded, with the process behind each
.PP
Usage: \fBfil ports [OPTIONS] [HOST] [SESSION]\fR
.TP
\fIHOST\fR
The host to ask. Left out inside a session: this machine\*(Aqs daemon, for this session
.TP
\fISESSION\fR
Only this session
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print JSON (schema 1) instead of the table
.TP
\fB\-\-probe\fR
Also probe the listeners no fil session started (by default only the \-L targets and the sessions\*(Aq listeners are). Such a listener is often a tunnel, and the probe reaches whatever it leads to; a known forwarder (ssh, kubectl, ...) is never probed
.SS "fil server"
The remote side: run by the pipe command on the host, not by people
.PP
Usage: \fBfil server \-\-stdio\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-stdio\fR
Relay this process\*(Aqs stdin and stdout to the daemon (starting it if needed)
.SS "fil install"
Install this fil on HOST over ssh, into ~/.local/share/fil (or check what is there, or remove what fil installed)
.PP
Usage: \fBfil install [OPTIONS] <HOST>\fR
.TP
\fIHOST\fR
The host: a name or alias for ssh, or user@name
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR
Show what is installed on HOST, and the fil on its PATH; change nothing
.TP
\fB\-\-remove\fR
Remove the copies fil installed on HOST (not one in use by a running daemon)
.TP
\fB\-\-binary\fR \fIFILE\fR
Upload this file instead of the bundled or this fil\*(Aqs own binary (it must be a release build for the host)
.SS "fil config"
Look at the configuration
.PP
Usage: \fBfil config [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>\fR
.SH NOTES
.nf
Examples:
  fil devbox                 attach to (or create) session "main" on devbox
  fil devbox build           attach to session "build"
  fil ls devbox              list the sessions on devbox
  fil kill devbox build      end session "build"
  fil detach devbox build    detach whoever is attached to "build"
.fi
.PP
Inside a session, type ~. after a newline to detach; ~? lists the escapes.
.PP
Configuration: ~/.config/fil/config.toml (see DESIGN.md section 14).
Logs: ~/.local/state/fil/client.log (set FIL_LOG=debug for more).
.PP
Exit status: 0 on a normal end (detached, or the session ended with nothing to report);
the shell\*(Aqs own status if it exited with one; 128+N if the shell was killed by signal N or
fil was stopped by signal N;
1 on an error or if the session was taken over, replaced or detached elsewhere;
2 on a usage error.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBfil\-config\fR(5), \fBfil\-policy\fR(5)
